/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Remote sensing measures an object from a distance, without contact.
   - Electromagnetic radiation (EMR) is a pair of fields, one electric and one magnetic, oscillating at right angles and travelling forward together.
   - c is the speed of light in m/s; f is frequency in Hz; λ is wavelength in m.
   - A photon is one quantum of EMR.
   - The electromagnetic spectrum is the full range of EMR, ordered by wavelength.
   - Gamma rays and X-rays are ionising and have very short wavelengths.
   - Two natural sources drive most passive remote sensing.
   - Radiometry names the energy at each stage.
   - Ep is photon energy in electron volts; λ is wavelength in micrometres.
   - Photon energy falls as wavelength rises.
*/
