/* book exam traps: Wavelength and frequency move in opposite directions. A longer wavelength means a lower frequency and a lower energy per photon. Photon energy is not beam power: a warm surface sends very many weak infrared photons, so the infrared beam can still carry a lot o | The wave equation is c = f λ, not c = f ÷ λ. Rearrange to f = c ÷ λ and λ = c ÷ f. Check units: Hz × m = m/s. | A micrometre is 10⁻⁶ m, not 10⁻³ m. Write 0.55 µm as 0.55 × 10⁻⁶ m before dividing. Mixing µm with m is the most common numerical slip. | Irradiance is flux divided by area. The same 100 W spread over 2 m² gives 50 W/m², over 4 m² only 25 W/m²; a larger area lowers the irradiance, it never raises it. | Near infrared, 0.7–1.3 µm, is mostly reflected sunlight. Thermal infrared, 8–14 µm, is emitted by the target. Do not call a 10 µm image a reflected infrared image; and in Wien's law use kelvin, never degrees Celsius. */
