/* book exam traps: Radiance is not reflectance. Radiance changes with sun angle, haze and viewing direction even when the ground is the same; reflectance changes only when the surface itself changes, such as a growing crop, wet soil or fresh snow. Convert to reflectance before c | Rayleigh scattering goes as 1/λ⁴, not 1/λ, and short wavelengths scatter most. The sky is blue because blue is scattered out of the beam, not because blue is absorbed. | Albedo is a broadband average, never the reflectance of a single band. Quoting a one-band value as albedo loses marks. | Path radiance adds to the signal, so an uncorrected hazy image gives a reflectance that is too high (0.173 against 0.157 in the worked example). Subtracting Lp but forgetting the transmittance overshoots the other way. | For an opaque surface τ = 0, so ρ + α = 1. Do not set τ = 1: that would describe a perfectly transparent surface that reflects and absorbs nothing. */
